codex
Implemented and verified a Track B generator based on the paper’s subtrace/minimal-polynomial identity.

Key results:

- Shipping preset: `easy`, `n=4096`
- Local gates: all pass
- Bare oracle hardening: `0/3` solved
- G9: bare `0/3`, structural `1/3`, placebo `0/3`
- Structure-aware guess probability: `1/7,340,033`
- Reference cost: 20,508 modular operations
- Compact route: at most 60 operations
- Answer size: 46 characters, six atoms

Deliverables:

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2405.11463/gen_2405_11463.py)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2405.11463/README.md)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2405.11463/selftest_report.json)
- [Bare hardening transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2405.11463/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2405.11463/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2405.11463/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The README clearly notes that this covers the [paper’s](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.11463) native subtrace machinery, not its headline primitive-normal-pair search.
